# Cairn v3 server flags. Install to /etc/cairn/server.env (root:cairn 0640).
#
# systemd word-splits a bare $CAIRN_ARGS, so the whole flag list fits in one
# variable. Keep it on a single line; a trailing backslash is not continued here
# the way a shell would continue it.

# ─── mTLS, the intended configuration ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The device verifies the server against a CA pinned in firmware, and presents
# its own certificate whose CommonName must equal its 32-hex device id. The
# server compares that name to the device id inside the signed manifest and
# refuses a mismatch, so a stolen card cannot upload as a different device.
#
# This is why the ingest listener is not behind a reverse proxy. The handler
# reads r.TLS.PeerCertificates to do that comparison; terminating TLS upstream
# makes r.TLS nil and reduces the check to trusting a forwarded header, which
# anything able to reach the backend could forge.

# -addr names LOOPBACK, not the wildcard :8443. On a host running
# `tailscale serve --tcp 8443` (which is how the dongle reaches this server over LTE),
# tailscaled holds the tailnet addresses for 8443 and forwards to tcp://localhost:8443.
# A wildcard bind loses that race and the unit crash-loops with
# "listen tcp :8443: bind: address already in use". See docs/deploying.md.
CAIRN_ARGS=-addr 127.0.0.1:8443 -data /var/lib/cairn -tls-cert /etc/cairn/certs/server.pem -tls-key /etc/cairn/certs/server-key.pem -tls-client-ca /etc/cairn/certs/ca.pem

# ─── plaintext, for a device that cannot do mTLS yet ─────────────────────────
#
# A device needs two things before it can speak mTLS, and both require physical
# access: CAIRN_SERVER_CA_PEM compiled into firmware, and client.crt/client.key
# placed in /cairn/certs/ on its card. Until then it talks plain HTTP on :8080
# and this is the only configuration it can reach.
#
# Not as bad as it sounds, and not an excuse either: a receipt's Ed25519
# signature is what authorizes deleting data, not the transport, so plaintext
# changes who can read an upload rather than whether a deletion is legitimate.
#
# CAIRN_ARGS=-dev -addr :8080 -data /var/lib/cairn

# ─── the iOS app API, optional ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A SEPARATE listener from the device's mTLS port: the device listener demands a
# client certificate at the handshake, which a phone cannot present (and over
# Tailscale could not, because tailscale serve terminates TLS on the host). The
# app authenticates with per-request signatures instead. See
# contracts/sync/v1/spec.md and docs/tailscale-deployment.md.
#
# One process serves both routes from one API instance, so the LAN and the tailnet
# share a single sync log, cursor and set of clients (two processes would each
# open the log and corrupt it):
#
#   -app-addr :8444                      LAN, TLS, reuses -tls-cert/-tls-key
#   -app-serve-addr 127.0.0.1:8445       loopback plain HTTP, for tailscale serve
#   -trust-tailscale-serve               honour Serve's identity headers from loopback
#   -app-snapshot-url http://127.0.0.1:8480   authenticated /v1/snapshot proxy
#
# then: tailscale serve --bg --https=443 http://127.0.0.1:8445
#
# Full line:
#   CAIRN_ARGS=-addr 127.0.0.1:8443 -data /var/lib/cairn -tls-cert ... -tls-key ... -tls-client-ca ... -app-addr :8444 -app-serve-addr 127.0.0.1:8445 -trust-tailscale-serve -app-snapshot-url http://127.0.0.1:8480
#
# The web UI's server side asks for vehicle display names on a third, loopback-only
# listener with no authentication of its own (the loopback bind is the control):
#   -app-local-addr 127.0.0.1:8446
# and the UI is told where with NUXT_CAIRN_LOCAL_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8446. NEVER
# point tailscale serve or a proxy at it; it refuses requests carrying Tailscale
# identity headers as a backstop, but the placement is the actual control.
#
# Optional extra rule: tailnet requests must also carry your Tailscale identity.
#   -require-tailnet-identity -tailnet-allow-login you@example.com
#
# Never run `tailscale funnel` for this service; the listener rejects requests
# carrying the Funnel marker, but not enabling Funnel is the actual control.

# ─── OTA, optional ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Append -firmware-dir /var/lib/cairn/firmware to serve signed update
# descriptors. Omitting it disables the OTA endpoints entirely rather than
# serving an empty list, so a misconfigured path cannot look like "no update".
